{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Carve for yourself two stone tablets like the first ones, and I will write.</b> The sense is: 'and I will write with My finger,' for it is written (Exodus 32) 'by the finger of Elohim.' This is the meaning of 'like the first ones, and I will write' — the Writing says: the writing shall be a writing by the finger of Elohim like the first tablets." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> 'Carve for yourself two stone tablets' — this is what Scripture says (Ecclesiastes 3): 'a time to cast away stones and a time to gather stones.' 'A time to cast away stones' — when he cast the tablets from his hands; 'and a time to gather stones' — 'carve for yourself two stone tablets.' This is what Scripture says (Proverbs 10): 'The blessing of YHWH, it makes rich' — this is the blessing of Moshe, when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Carve for yourself,' and He showed him a quarry of sapphire inside his tent, and he carved from it, and the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: 'Carve for yourself — the surplus is yours.' And from there he became rich and was made king. From this you learn that all who engage in Torah, their sustenance comes from the Torah, as it is written (Joshua 1): 'for then you shall make your way prosperous and then you shall have good success' (Proverbs 10:22), 'and He adds no sorrow with it' — that he did not need to trouble himself to go to another place, but from within his tent the Holy One, blessed be He, prepared his sustenance for him. Another interpretation: 'Carve for yourself' — a parable of a king who went to a province across the sea and left his wife with the maidservants. Because she was with them, an evil report went out about her. The king heard and sought to kill her. Her companion heard and tore up her marriage contract. He said: if the king says, 'my wife did such and such,' I will say to him, 'she is not yet your wife.' The king investigated her and found no fault in her — it was only from the maidservants. Immediately he was reconciled. Her companion said to him: 'My lord the king, write her another marriage contract, since the first was torn.' He said to him: 'You tore it — you bring the paper from your own, and I will write it in my own hand.' Therefore it says: 'Carve for yourself.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:06:52.916502518Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}